This paper investigates a decentralized multi-agent resource allocation problem, where agents autonomously make decisions to efficiently distribute resources, leveraging only local market-clearing conditions without requiring a central authority. The proposed framework enables agents to reach an equilibrium where overall utility is maximized, closely approximating full information settings, all while maintaining simplicity and ease of implementation. The approach is further generalized to accommodate varying levels of coordination among agents through utility function design, demonstrating robustness in the presence of noisy utilities and an agent's initial preferences. The proposed strategy is also shown to be effective in a dynamic real-world setting, utilizing a dataset from a real-world electricity market to illustrate its potential. The analysis highlights the advantages of the proposed framework, showing that it outperforms a best-case scenario and compares favorably with existing methods in certain situations. The code used for experimentation is publicly available. 
